# XCU ps
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The default output format is unspecified; the tests use -o (headers and
# value shapes in the POSIX locale are specified) and select the shell that
# runs the test and a sleeping child it starts. XSI-shaded options are in
# their own assertion.

assertion  XCU.ps.001
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       ps -o pid= -p PID writes one line holding only the process ID, for
           the shell that runs the test (no header line because the header
           text is null).
test       t/001-pid-only.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.002
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       In the POSIX locale the -o names pid, ppid, pgid, user, ruser,
           group, rgroup, nice, vsz, etime, time, tty, pcpu, comm and args
           give the headings PID, PPID, PGID, USER, RUSER, GROUP, RGROUP, NI,
           VSZ, ELAPSED, TIME, TT, %CPU, COMMAND and COMMAND, and a list of
           names may be separated by blanks or commas.
test       t/002-default-headers.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.003
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       name=text replaces the heading; when every heading is null no
           heading line is written; several -o options act as one list in the
           order given; the fields come in the order listed.
           Widened in the hardening round, from the same passage on all-null
           headings: when only some headings are null the heading line is still
           written.
test       t/003-header-text-and-order.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.004
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       For a background child of the shell running the test, -p of its
           process ID selects it, pid shows that ID and ppid shows the shell's
           process ID.
test       t/004-child-ppid.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.005
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       The list argument of -p may be separated by commas or by blanks and
           then selects every process named.
test       t/005-proc-list.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.006
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       Several selection options select the inclusive OR of their
           criteria: -p of a child together with -U of the real user ID lists
           the shell that runs the test as well as the child.
           Widened in the hardening round, from the same inclusive-OR passage:
           the options may come in either order; where the suite has privilege,
           -p of a process of another user with -U of the invoker's real user
           ID lists that process and the invoker's.
test       t/006-selection-or.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.007
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       -A lists every process, including the shell that runs the test and
           its child; -U with the real user ID and -G with the real group ID
           list the shell that runs the test.
           Widened in the hardening round, from the OPTIONS passages for -A,
           -U and -G: where the suite has privilege and a process of another
           user (65534) exists, -A lists it; -U and -G with the invoker's IDs
           do not; with 65534 they list it and not the shell; lists separated by
           commas or blanks select every one named; -U accepts a login name.
test       t/007-select-all-user-group.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.008
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       -e lists the shell that runs the test, -u with the effective user
           ID lists it, -f gives the headings UID PID PPID C STIME TTY TIME CMD
           in that order, and -l gives F S UID PID PPID C PRI NI ADDR SZ WCHAN
           TTY TIME CMD in that order.
           Widened in the hardening round: where a process of another user
           exists, -e lists it and -u of the invoker's ID does not; -u of a
           list selects every user named and accepts a login name; -f writes
           the login name under UID and -l the numeric ID (STDOUT description
           of UID).
test       t/008-xsi-options.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.009
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       The user, ruser, group and rgroup fields of the shell running the
           test show the textual name or else the decimal ID of the effective
           user, real user, effective group and real group.
           Widened in the hardening round: where the suite has privilege, for a
           process whose effective user and group differ from its real ones the
           real fields show the invoker's and the effective fields do not.
test       t/009-user-group-fields.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.010
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       For a background child started as "sleep 301", comm shows sleep
           and args shows a command line that begins with sleep.
test       t/010-comm-args.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.011
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       In the POSIX locale etime has the form [[dd-]hh:]mm:ss and time
           the form [dd-]hh:mm:ss, nice and vsz are decimal integers and
           pcpu a decimal number; a hyphen may stand for a value that is not
           available.
test       t/011-value-shapes.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.012
anchor     utilities/ps.html#DESCRIPTION
class      untestable
says       With no selection option ps lists the processes that have the
           same effective user ID and the same controlling terminal as the
           invoker.
reason     A script cannot give the test a controlling terminal of its own,
           and without one the set of processes selected is not defined.

assertion  XCU.ps.013
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS/-a
class      required
since      1992
says       On a terminal, ps -a lists a process associated with that
           terminal that is not a session leader.
           Widened in the hardening round, from "all processes associated with
           terminals": a process that has no terminal is not listed.
test       t/013-a-terminal.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.014
anchor     utilities/ps.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With no selection option, run on a terminal, ps lists processes
           with the invoker's effective user ID and controlling terminal,
           including the invoking shell and its background children.
           Widened in the hardening round, from the default-selection sentence:
           a process without that terminal, or of another effective user, is
           not listed.
test       t/014-default-selection-terminal.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.015
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       In the default listing, a process that has exited and has not been
           waited for by its parent is marked defunct.
test       t/015-defunct.sh

assertion  XCU.ps.016
anchor     utilities/ps.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ps.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/ps.html#APPLICATION_USAGE
class      required
says       The text after the equals sign of an -o name is the whole rest of
           the argument as the heading, so it may contain blanks: a heading
           "Process ID" or "User Name" is written as given. (Added in the
           hardening round; the page's own usage note shows such headings.)
test       t/016-header-text-blank.sh
